        {
            "number": 2,
            "revision": "31e6af919273b11d3fe37002ccbe62907ef96c14",
            "parents": [
                "5bffc3aeba5fcce359d0b92cf611df19435dc805"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/17/88217/2",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Ed Tanous",
                "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                "username": "edtanous"
            },
            "createdOn": 1773678422,
            "author": {
                "name": "Ed Tanous",
                "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                "username": "edtanous"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Myung Bae",
                        "email": "myungbae@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "baemyung"
                    },
                    "message": "We're currently working to make cppcheck more useful to meet our static code security scan requirement.\nLater, we also intend to push upstream.\nSo, I vote to keep cppcheck."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> Later, we also intend to push upstream.\n\nCould we remove for now, and when it's useful, we add it back?\n\nFWIW, I've worked at several companies that have static code security scanning requirements.  cpplint did not meet the minimums so I'm worried about being stuck forever seeing warnings.  If we can make it useful, I'm happy to have it back later if that's ok?\n\nThose minimums is why for some of the repos I maintain I run coverity separately once a month: https://scan.coverity.com/projects/openbmc-bmcweb?tab=overview\n\nThis isn't an advertisement for coverity.  I run several scanners, and really any that have good open source policies are welcome, this is just one that some companies use so it's good to be able to diff upstream and downstream."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Do you want to enable it for every repo?\n\nIf you are doing it for security purpose then ipmi + bmcweb (redfish) would be most critical so i can imagine the focus would be on those repos and their dependencies.\n\nIs it possible to enable it only for some repos which are being enabled and then roll it out over time?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> Do you want to enable it for every repo?\n\nI'm happy to have this discussion but cpplint has had these warnings for years at this point and no action that I can see, so I'd prefer we have this discussion on another patch, ideally the one Myung is referring to where they're working on making it useful.\n\n2 years ago, I tried to make a way for repos to opt in:\nhttps://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/openbmc-build-scripts/+/60863\n\nAnd there was no input.  If someone wants to fix this and puts forward real effort, this 44 lines of patch won't be a huge impetiment.  I will personally revert this patch if someone puts forward that level of effort, but for now, I don't see it.  On bmcweb, cppcheck warnings are about 40% of the logging output, and as a maintainer get in the way of triaging real failures.\n\nI would like to see this patch merge."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Myung Bae",
                        "email": "myungbae@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "baemyung"
                    },
                    "message": ">Could we remove for now, and when it's useful, we add it back?\nI'm good with this.\n\nNot only for cppcheck, we are also exploring whether some other tools/options may be compliant with our approved requirements.\n\nThanks for the link."
                }
            ],
